DISCUSSION
Anti-GAD antibodies are implicated in a number of neurologic syndromes, including SPS, CA,
and LE. High titers have also been detected in some patients with treatment-resistant localization-
related epilepsy.1 Anti-GAD antibody is detected in 80% of patients with newly diagnosed
DM1, although at lower titers and against different epitopes from those seen in neurologic
disease.2 Patients with neurologic manifestations of elevated anti-GAD levels are more likely to
have organ-specific autoantibodies, including anti-TPO (occurring in 50% –60% of patients),
anti-gliadin, and others.3,4 Other autoimmune conditions such as pernicious anemia and pso-
riasis have been reported. 3,4 Our patient had both personal and family history of several auto-
immune diseases, in keeping with previously described associations.
The pathogenic role of anti-GAD antibodies in neurologic disease is unclear.2,4 Titers of anti-
GAD antibody do not necessarily correlate with the severity of neurologic manifestations. 5 In
our patient, there was at least a modest correlation between titers and clinical improvement in
response to immunomodulation, both during his initial presentation with SPS and CA and
during his episode of LE. Unfortunately, the titers during these 2 episodes were reported from
different labs using different techniques, and there is no way to directly compare them.
Combinations of the major features of anti-GAD antibody syndrome —DM1, SPS, CA,
seizures, and LE —have been reported in individual patients, but our patient presented with
all of these clinical syndromes.
There is no consensus about appropriate treatment for these patients. Case reports and series
have reported variable responses to corticosteroids, IVIg, plasmapheresis, rituximab, and cyclo-
phosphamide. There is one randomized trial of IVIg for the treatment of SPS that demonstrat-
ed efficacy compared with placebo, 6 but there are no randomized trials for treatment of other
disorders associated with anti-GAD antibodies. LE associated with anti-GAD antibodies may
